{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Oxygen vacancies and Ti³⁺ states in reduced anatase TiO₂ thin films probed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A. Surface¹, B. Vacuum¹, C. Synchrotron² — ¹Department of Materials, ²Diamond Light Source",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Anatase TiO₂ films grown by atomic layer deposition were annealed in ultra-high vacuum between 300 and 700 °C and analysed in situ by monochromated Al Kα XPS. Peak fitting of the Ti 2p region with spin–orbit-constrained doublets on a Shirley background resolves a Ti³⁺ component shifted by −1.7 eV from Ti⁴⁺, whose fraction rises from 2 % to 14 % with annealing temperature. The O 1s spectra show a concurrent growth of a hydroxyl / defect component at 531.3 eV. An Arrhenius analysis yields an apparent activation energy of 0.62 ± 0.05 eV for vacancy formation.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "XPS · TiO₂ · oxygen vacancies · peak fitting · Shirley background",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Keywords"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Titanium dioxide is the archetypal photocatalyst, and its activity is governed to a large extent by point defects at the surface ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "diebold2003"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Removal of lattice oxygen leaves two excess electrons that localise on neighbouring Ti sites, producing Ti³⁺ centres with a characteristic chemical shift in the Ti 2p spectrum ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "biesinger2010"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Quantifying that component reliably requires a physically constrained fit; in this work we describe a protocol and apply it to a temperature series.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Experimental",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Sample preparation",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Films of nominal thickness 30 nm were grown on Si(100) by thermal ALD from TiCl₄ and H₂O at 250 °C (600 cycles) and crystallised to anatase by a 1 h anneal in air at 450 °C, confirmed by Raman (144 cm⁻¹ E_g mode).",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "XPS acquisition",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Monochromated Al Kα (hν = 1486.6 eV), 400 µm spot",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pass energy 20 eV for core levels, 150 eV for surveys",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Charge neutraliser on; energy scale referenced to adventitious C 1s at 284.8 eV",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Base pressure < 5 × 10⁻¹⁰ mbar during acquisition",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Peak fitting",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Each component was modelled with a pseudo-Voigt line shape, a Gaussian–Lorentzian product with mixing parameter ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\eta",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "I(E) = A\\left[\\eta\\,\\frac{1}{1+4\\left(\\frac{E-E_0}{\\beta}\\right)^2} + (1-\\eta)\\,\\exp\\!\\left(-4\\ln 2\\left(\\frac{E-E_0}{\\beta}\\right)^2\\right)\\right]",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:pv",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The inelastic background was removed with the iterative Shirley algorithm ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "shirley1972"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ", in which the background at energy ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "E",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is proportional to the integrated peak area at lower binding energy:",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "B(E) = B_{\\text{low}} + (B_{\\text{high}}-B_{\\text{low}})\\,\\frac{\\int_{E}^{E_{\\text{high}}} [I(E')-B(E')]\\,dE'}{\\int_{E_{\\text{low}}}^{E_{\\text{high}}} [I(E')-B(E')]\\,dE'}.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:shirley",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "For the Ti 2p doublet the 2p₁/₂ component was tied to 2p₃/₂ with a spin–orbit splitting of 5.72 eV and an area ratio fixed at the degeneracy ratio",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\frac{A(2p_{1/2})}{A(2p_{3/2})} = \\frac{2j_{1/2}+1}{2j_{3/2}+1} = \\frac{1}{2}.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:ratio",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Atomic concentrations follow from the fitted areas and the Scofield relative sensitivity factors ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "S_i",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "x_i = \\frac{A_i / S_i}{\\sum_j A_j / S_j}.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:quant",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:ti2p",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " lists the fitted Ti 2p₃/₂ parameters. The Ti⁴⁺ position is stable to within 0.05 eV across the series, confirming that charge referencing is not responsible for the apparent new component.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Anneal (°C)",
          "Ti⁴⁺ BE (eV)",
          "Ti³⁺ BE (eV)",
          "FWHM Ti⁴⁺ (eV)",
          "Ti³⁺ fraction (\\%)"
        ],
        [
          "as-grown",
          "458.72",
          "—",
          "0.98",
          "< 1"
        ],
        [
          "300",
          "458.70",
          "457.02",
          "0.99",
          "2.1"
        ],
        [
          "400",
          "458.69",
          "457.00",
          "1.01",
          "4.8"
        ],
        [
          "500",
          "458.68",
          "456.98",
          "1.03",
          "7.9"
        ],
        [
          "600",
          "458.66",
          "456.97",
          "1.06",
          "11.2"
        ],
        [
          "700",
          "458.65",
          "456.95",
          "1.09",
          "14.0"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Ti 2p₃/₂ fit results (Shirley background, pseudo-Voigt, η = 0.3).",
      "label": "tab:ti2p",
      "alignment": "lrrrr",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The O 1s envelope requires three components: lattice O²⁻ at 530.0 eV, OH / O-vacancy-adjacent at 531.3 eV and adsorbed H₂O at 532.6 eV (Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:o1s",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ").",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Component",
          "BE (eV)",
          "FWHM (eV)",
          "Area 300 °C (\\%)",
          "Area 700 °C (\\%)"
        ],
        [
          "O–Ti (lattice)",
          "530.0",
          "1.10",
          "88.5",
          "79.2"
        ],
        [
          "OH / defect",
          "531.3",
          "1.45",
          "9.1",
          "18.3"
        ],
        [
          "H₂O (ads.)",
          "532.6",
          "1.60",
          "2.4",
          "2.5"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "O 1s component analysis.",
      "label": "tab:o1s",
      "alignment": "lrrrr",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Discussion",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Assuming the Ti³⁺ fraction is proportional to the equilibrium vacancy concentration, an Arrhenius plot of ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\ln x_{\\mathrm{Ti^{3+}}}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " against ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "1/T",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is linear (",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R^2 = 0.991",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ") with slope",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\frac{d\\ln x}{d(1/T)} = -\\frac{E_a}{k_B}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad E_a = 0.62 \\pm 0.05\\ \\text{eV}.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:arrhenius",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This value is smaller than the bulk vacancy formation energy predicted by hybrid DFT (≈ 4 eV), as expected for a surface process assisted by the reducing UHV environment and by the entropy of released O₂.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The information depth of the measurement, taken as three inelastic mean free paths, is",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "d = 3\\lambda\\cos\\theta \\approx 3 \\times 2.1\\ \\text{nm} \\times \\cos 0^\\circ \\approx 6\\ \\text{nm},",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:depth",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "so the Ti³⁺ signal originates from the outermost ~20 atomic layers. Angle-resolved measurements would discriminate surface from sub-surface vacancies.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Conclusions",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "A constrained Ti 2p fit reliably separates Ti³⁺ at −1.7 eV from Ti⁴⁺.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Ti³⁺ fraction rises monotonically to 14 % at 700 °C.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Vacancy formation has an apparent activation energy of 0.62 eV.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
